    isbefore: []  # e4d7
    duplicate_of: []  # e4d7
    not_duplicate_of: []  # e4d7
    pr_ref:  # e4d7
    description: "## Statement (also in `fields.statement`)\n\nHypergumbo runs multiple passes that each enumerate \"what's in the file set\" or \"what's an entrypoint\": the language detector, the analyzer dispatcher, the profile reporter, the metrics reporter, the entrypoint detector. When two passes claim different cardinalities OR different members on identical input, exactly one of two things must be true:\n\n- (a) The divergence is **principled and documented** (e.g., \"pass X counts source files only; pass Y counts all files including tests; here's where to look for the policy\"), OR\n- (b) The passes must **reconcile to a shared set** (single source of truth for the file enumeration).\n\nToday, divergences exist silently — neither documented nor reconciled. Two passes invoked on the same repo emit different counts of \"bash files\", \"total files\", or \"entrypoints\" with no signal to the consumer about which is canonical.\n\n## Root cause (also in `fields.root_cause`)\n\nEach pass implements its own file or entrypoint enumeration without consulting a shared source of truth. Three mechanisms produce the disagreements:\n\n1. **Taxonomy aliasing collapses one set into two** — `bash` and `shell` are alias keys in the language profile table, so a single `.sh` file gets counted under both (INV-tosum).\n2. **Inconsistent exclude policies across passes** — the language profile and the analyzer dispatcher apply different filters (one excludes tests, the other doesn't; one walks symlinks, the other doesn't), producing different file counts on the same input (INV-hokig).\n3. **Ad-hoc deduplication logic** — entrypoint detection produces one entry per detection signal rather than one entry per script, so a script with both `if __name__ == \"__main__\"` AND a `main()` function appears twice (INV-hosuh).\n\nThe semantic-ambiguity case (WI-soraj: 3 different `total_files` values) is the surface symptom of all three mechanisms.\n\n## Members\n\n- `INV-tosum` (P2 violated) — Language profile double-counts shell scripts as both `bash` and `shell` due to alias-key duplication. Taxonomy aliasing.\n- `INV-hokig` (P2 violated, also tagged `cross_language_parity`) — Profile and analyzer pass disagree on bash file count (10 vs 41 on self-analysis) due to inconsistent exclude policy. Pass-policy divergence.\n- `WI-soraj` (P2 todo_hard) — `metrics.total_files` has 3 different values on identical input (863 vs 1119 vs 809). Semantic ambiguity manifested at the reporting layer.\n- `INV-hosuh` (P2 violated) — Entrypoint detection double-counts scripts with both `if __name__ == \"__main__\"` AND a `main()` function (17 entries from 11 distinct scripts). Ad-hoc deduplication failure.\n\n## Why this is an invariant, not four independent fixes\n\nEach member fixes the disagreement at one pass-boundary, but none of the four members alone addresses the underlying question: *which pass owns \"the file set\", and which passes consume from it?* If INV-tosum is fixed (bash/shell alias collapsed), INV-hokig's profile-vs-analyzer disagreement might still exist for other languages. If INV-hokig is fixed (profile and analyzer reconciled for bash), WI-soraj's three-different-totals might still exist at the metrics layer because metrics computes its own count.\n\nThe shared fix shape is **declare a canonical file enumerator (or set of canonical enumerators with documented per-purpose differences) and route every count through it**. Whether the canonical enumerator lives in `profile.py`, `analyze/discovery.py`, or a new shared module is the architectural decision this META forces.\n\n## Cross-language note\n\nThis axis interacts with **cross-language parity** (the `cross_language_parity` tag). The disagreements surface most visibly for bash (10 vs 41) because bash is a second-class analysis target; Python's file count is well-bounded because the Python analyzer dominates the pipeline. Fixing this META should produce by-language file-set claims that consumers (especially `dead-code-maybe`, `slice`, `io-boundaries`) can rely on uniformly.\n\n## Closure criterion\n\nA canonical file/script enumerator (or small documented set of enumerators) exists, every pass that emits a file-count or entrypoint-count routes through it, and either (a) all passes report the same count for the same input OR (b) each pass documents its policy at the reporting site. Member items close as their specific bug is reconciled; META closes when the discipline is in place.\n\n## Related but separate\n\n- **Identity META** (`INV-dajaf`): about Symbol identity claims. This META is about set-membership claims. Structurally analogous (both are \"honesty about what's in a collection\") but distinct axes.\n- **Schema-spec drift META**: about declared-but-unimplemented contracts. This META is about declared-and-implemented passes that disagree among themselves. Different shape."  # e4d7
    fields:  # e4d7
      statement: "Hypergumbo runs multiple passes that each enumerate 'what's in the file set' or 'what's an entrypoint'. When two passes claim different cardinalities OR different members on identical input, exactly one of two things must be true: (a) the divergence is principled and documented (e.g., 'pass X counts source files only; pass Y counts all files including tests'), OR (b) the passes must reconcile to a shared set (single source of truth). Today, divergences exist silently — neither documented nor reconciled."  # e4d7
      root_cause: "Each pass implements its own file or entrypoint enumeration without consulting a shared source of truth. Three mechanisms produce the disagreements: (1) taxonomy aliasing collapses one set into two (bash/shell alias keys → INV-tosum); (2) inconsistent exclude policies across passes produce different counts on the same input (profile vs analyzer disagree → INV-hokig); (3) ad-hoc deduplication logic produces one entry per detection signal rather than one per script (entrypoints → INV-hosuh). The semantic-ambiguity case (3 different total_files values → WI-soraj) is the surface symptom of all three mechanisms at the reporting layer."  # e4d7
